From 7cc9f762f3b7606c443b9c228c2c6f64582ceaf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Nelson <will.nelson@discordapp.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:51:02 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] plugin-runtime: meter parse work in query fuel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0006 bounded the tree-sitter parse that set_text runs, but in
parser operations, at 10 per byte of each call's own input (at least
10,000), with no way for the caller to size or see the budget. That has
three problems:

- It's a separate currency from query fuel, so a caller can't bound a
  document's total work with one pool.
- It's per call, so a document holding N injected blocks gets N budgets,
  and the same bytes re-parsed at every level of an injection chain each
  get their own allotment too.
- It scales with input length, so a large document buys proportionally
  more parse time — and operations aren't uniformly priced: one taken in
  error recovery costs 5–10× an ordinary one.

Meter the parse in the same fuel queries burn, with a caller-supplied
allotment:

- set_text_with_fuel(session_id, text, fuel) -> FuelSpent parses on an
  explicit allotment and reports what the parse cost and whether it was
  cut off. On every tick of the parse progress callback (every 100
  operations) it prices the parse so far with parse_fuel_cost():

      PARSE_FUEL_PER_OPERATION        (16) × parser operations
    + PARSE_FUEL_PER_REDUCTION_SCAN   (15) × reduction_scan_count
    + PARSE_FUEL_PER_POPPED_SUBTREE   (47) × popped_subtree_count
    + PARSE_FUEL_PER_LEXED_CHAR       (10) × lexed_char_count
    + PARSE_FUEL_PER_SUMMARIZED_CHILD  (2) × summarized_child_count

  using the counters the previous patch publishes on TSParseState, and
  halts the parser once the allotment is spent. Because the result is in
  query-fuel units, a caller can thread one pool through every parse and
  every query (pool -= spent.used).
- set_text runs on DEFAULT_PARSE_FUEL (80e6, the same figure as
  DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL). PARSE_OPERATIONS_PER_BYTE and MIN_PARSE_OPERATIONS
  are removed. apply_edit's incremental re-parse goes through the same
  metered path instead of running unbounded.
- The session records its last parse's cost; parse_fuel(session_id)
  reads it, so a caller that parsed at set_text time can charge it to a
  pool later.
- A parse that ran out still leaves no tree, and the parser is still
  reset so the next parse starts fresh. parse_with_fuel /
  parse_utf16_with_fuel now report that session as out of fuel with an
  empty result, rather than as a "no text set" error, so a starved parse
  surfaces through the same out_of_fuel signal as a starved query.
  Sessions that were never given text still error.

Calibration. Each input below was parsed natively to count operations,
reduction scans, popped subtrees, lexed characters and summarized
children (the counts are deterministic: fuel computed from the native
counts matches the wasm runtime's exactly), then timed in the browser
runtime (V8, the emscripten host built with emsdk 5.0.6) with the pool
lifted, so the attacks ran to completion. The set is ordinary source,
broken and wrong-language source, the recovery attacks, and lexer-heavy
input (long strings, comments, identifiers and whitespace runs,
unterminated strings and templates, long Markdown paragraphs and code
fences). A non-negative least-squares fit of wasm time on the five
counts, weighted by relative error, gives ~39 ns per operation, ~39 ns
per reduction scan, ~118 ns per popped subtree, ~24 ns per lexed
character and ~5.5 ns per summarized child. Query fuel costs ~2.5 ns per
unit on the same runtime for large ordinary files (0.5–3.2 across the
inputs), so dividing by 2.5 gives the constants above.

  input                           ms   parse fuel   ns/fuel
  TypeScript, 808 KB            63.7      28.0e6      2.28
  Markdown (CLAUDE.md), 38 KB    8.1       3.1e6      2.57
  Kotlin, ordinary, 24 KB        7.6       2.7e6      2.80
  Kotlin `a.b()` chain, 16 KB   13.7       5.4e6      2.54
  TypeScript, every 37th char
    dropped, 79 KB              23.6       8.5e6      2.78
  Kotlin, every 23rd dropped    24.2       7.0e6      3.43
  Rust parsed as TypeScript     15.1       5.2e6      2.94
  Markdown parsed as JS         10.6       4.7e6      2.24
  Markdown, 200 KB code fence  133.2      53.9e6      2.47
  Markdown, 200 KB paragraph    43.1      16.1e6      2.68
  TS, 5,000 unterminated
    string lines                56.8      30.3e6      1.87
  JS, unterminated 50 KB
    template literal          1991.9     734.6e6      2.71
  Kotlin `-}` × 500, 1 KB      181.7      80.0e6      2.27
  Kotlin `-}` × 2000, 4 KB    2695.8    1250.1e6      2.16

Every input of a millisecond or more that ticks at least once lands at
1.87–3.98 ns per fuel, within 0.73–1.57× of the model. Without the
summarized-children term the template literal is priced at ~1/7 of its
time and the spread is ~9.6×; without the lexed-character term the rest
fit about as well, the term mostly moving cost from operations onto
characters. Charging operations alone leaves ~8× between ordinary source
and recovery-heavy input.

With HIGHLIGHT_FUEL at 110e6, the two parse attacks above are cut off at
~250 ms (`-}`) and ~290 ms (the template literal).

Not covered: a document that parses in fewer than 100 operations never
ticks, so nothing is charged; a single 200 KB string, comment or
whitespace run is one such, at 1–5 ms, linear in its length.
